WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:12.877
stared at the blank piece of paper in front of him and sighed.

2
00:00:12.897 --> 00:00:15.738
"Wetlands are useless.

3
00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:18.199
I don't want to write a report on them!

4
00:00:18.639 --> 00:00:20.320
I'd rather hang out in the backyard."

5
00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:23.261
He grabbed his phone and headed outside.

6
00:00:23.762 --> 00:00:26.603
Will got comfortable in his favorite spot, the hammock.

7
00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:30.681
After a few minutes of rocking back and forth, he dozed off.

8
00:00:32.341 --> 00:00:34.123
Soon an annoying buzzing woke him up.

9
00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:36.864
"Stupid mosquitoes," Will grumbled.

10
00:00:36.884 --> 00:00:38.725
"I wish they'd all die."

11
00:00:39.265 --> 00:00:43.168
He opened his eyes and saw that he wasn't in the hammock anymore but in a canoe.

12
00:00:43.928 --> 00:00:46.290
And dozens of hungry mosquitoes were circling him.

13
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:53.594
"Actually if mosquitoes died, many other species would die too—ones you like," QT said.

14
00:00:53.614 --> 00:00:57.276
"Mosquitoes are an important part of the ecosystem."

15
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.021
Will got up and sat on the canoe's bench.

16
00:01:00.041 --> 00:01:01.422
"A part of what?"

17
00:01:01.442 --> 00:01:08.906
"An ecosystem is a community of plants and animals and the environment they live in," explained QT.

18
00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:14.009
"Each species within an ecosystem depends on the other species for its survival.

19
00:01:14.729 --> 00:01:18.752
If mosquitoes weren't around, the birds and fish that eat them would disappear.

20
00:01:19.472 --> 00:01:22.894
And then the species that eat those birds and fish would die off too."

21
00:01:22.914 --> 00:01:26.076
"I guess I don't want that Will said.

22
00:01:26.824 --> 00:01:29.646
"Each species is part of the ecosystem's food chain.

23
00:01:30.146 --> 00:01:32.448
Food chains show how species are connected.

24
00:01:33.268 --> 00:01:35.890
Algae and plants form the bottom of any food chain.

25
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.713
They're called producers because they can make their own food from sunlight.

26
00:01:41.453 --> 00:01:44.675
Creatures that eat other plants and animals are called consumers.

27
00:01:45.916 --> 00:01:48.818
Consumers that only eat plants are called herbivores.

28
00:01:49.358 --> 00:01:52.500
Those that eat both plants and animals are called omnivores.

29
00:01:53.288 --> 00:01:56.510
And those that only eat other animals are carnivores."

30
00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:58.811
Will peered into the water.

31
00:01:58.831 --> 00:02:00.251
"I see what you mean.

32
00:02:00.792 --> 00:02:02.513
Those little fish are eating plants.

33
00:02:03.273 --> 00:02:05.234
And that bird just ate one of the fish!

34
00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:10.056
And—whoa!—that alligator would have eaten the bird if he were a little quicker!"

35
00:02:10.076 --> 00:02:14.919
"Alligators are at the top of the food chain," QT said.

36
00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:17.660
"Very few species can prey on them.

37
00:02:18.444 --> 00:02:25.927
When alligators—and all other creatures—die, decomposers, like bacteria and fungi, break down the dead bodies.

38
00:02:26.768 --> 00:02:32.310
They return the energy and nutrients at the top of the food chain to the bottom so plants

39
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.611
have what they need to grow and start the whole cycle again."

40
00:02:35.631 --> 00:02:38.953
"There are so many animals here!

41
00:02:39.393 --> 00:02:42.334
There must be a lot of different food chains," Will guessed.

42
00:02:42.354 --> 00:02:43.755
"You're right.

43
00:02:44.335 --> 00:02:49.903
Some plants are eaten by mice, which are eaten by snakes, which are eaten by hawks.

44
00:02:50.723 --> 00:02:55.066
And some plants are eaten by deer, which are then eaten by panthers.

45
00:02:55.827 --> 00:03:01.731
The many connections between all the food chains in an ecosystem make up a food web."

46
00:03:01.752 --> 00:03:04.534
"Did you say 'panthers'?"

47
00:03:04.974 --> 00:03:06.155
Will asked in surprise.

48
00:03:06.175 --> 00:03:07.536
"Where are we?"

49
00:03:07.556 --> 00:03:12.720
"We are in the Everglades, a huge wetland in southern Florida.

50
00:03:13.424 --> 00:03:17.726
Right now we are in a freshwater marsh, a large part of the Everglades.

51
00:03:18.586 --> 00:03:22.028
Believe it or not, panthers used to be common around here."

52
00:03:22.528 --> 00:03:25.089
QT projected a panther sneaking up on a rabbit.

53
00:03:25.109 --> 00:03:29.150
"Now they're endangered and we're unlikely to see one."

54
00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:34.593
"Maybe we can see better from that tower," Will said, paddling the canoe toward it.

55
00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:36.713
"But what exactly is a wetland?"

56
00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:42.536
"It's just what it sounds like: an area that is covered by water for the majority of the

57
00:03:42.596 --> 00:03:47.329
time. Wetlands are pretty shallow, usually no more than a meter deep.

58
00:03:47.729 --> 00:03:49.310
There are two main types.

59
00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.631
Coastal wetlands—as you can probably guess—are close to a coast.

60
00:03:55.391 --> 00:04:01.193
Fresh water and salty water mix there, so only species that can tolerate water that's a little salty

61
00:04:01.253 --> 00:04:02.013
can survive.

62
00:04:02.734 --> 00:04:06.014
Inland wetlands are, well, farther inland.

63
00:04:06.575 --> 00:04:11.196
They usually form when rain floods a riverbed or another low, flat area.

64
00:04:11.944 --> 00:04:18.448
Wetlands exist on every continent, except for Antarctica, so there is a huge variety of different habitats.

65
00:04:19.488 --> 00:04:27.853
For instance, wetlands where mostly grasses grow—like this one—are called marshes, and those where trees grow are called

66
00:04:27.973 --> 00:04:33.676
swamps." The canoe reached the shore, and Will got out, stepping onto a path to the tower.

67
00:04:36.898 --> 00:04:40.520
When he reached the viewing platform, he said, "There's so much land!

68
00:04:40.896 --> 00:04:42.517
Why don't they do something useful with it?"

69
00:04:44.258 --> 00:04:45.699
"Wetlands are useful!"

70
00:04:46.099 --> 00:04:46.999
QT exclaimed.

71
00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:49.461
"They have several important jobs.

72
00:04:49.921 --> 00:04:51.842
For one they protect us from floods.

73
00:04:52.623 --> 00:04:59.426
When a heavy rainfall comes, wetlands act like a sponge, absorbing the extra water and releasing it slowly."

74
00:04:59.446 --> 00:05:01.988
"I guess that's useful," Will admitted.

75
00:05:02.008 --> 00:05:03.509
"What else do wetlands do?"

76
00:05:03.529 --> 00:05:09.132
"They clean water as it passes through, absorbing a lot of nutrients and pollutants.

77
00:05:10.992 --> 00:05:14.456
Let's continue to the coast, and you'll see another benefit of wetlands."

78
00:05:16.978 --> 00:05:22.283
As Will paddled along, the scenery began to change; the marsh became a maze of small islands and

79
00:05:22.343 --> 00:05:26.967
trees. "I've never seen trees growing in water before," commented Will.

80
00:05:26.987 --> 00:05:32.913
"We are in a mangrove swamp," said QT, "another habitat in the Everglades.

81
00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:40.833
It demonstrates another major role of wetlands: many species live here and migrating birds stop here to rest.

82
00:05:42.694 --> 00:05:47.995
Plant roots also keep the soil from being swept away by the flow of water and even make

83
00:05:48.035 --> 00:05:50.236
it possible for new land to build up."

84
00:05:52.056 --> 00:05:54.637
Will noticed something big and gray swimming nearby.

85
00:05:54.657 --> 00:05:55.657
"What's that?"

86
00:05:56.017 --> 00:05:56.457
he asked.

87
00:05:57.177 --> 00:06:01.519
QT scanned the underwater creature and replied, "That is a manatee.

88
00:06:02.199 --> 00:06:04.259
They're harmless herbivores; don't worry.

89
00:06:04.679 --> 00:06:06.760
They live close to the shore here in Florida.

90
00:06:07.564 --> 00:06:10.785
Sailors long ago thought these animals were mermaids."

91
00:06:12.485 --> 00:06:14.446
Will stopped paddling to watch the manatee.

92
00:06:14.466 --> 00:06:16.667
"It doesn't look like a mermaid to me!"

93
00:06:17.887 --> 00:06:20.428
Soon the gentle rocking of the boat put him to sleep.

94
00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:26.330
When he opened his eyes, he was back in the hammock, still surrounded by mosquitoes.

95
00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:29.350
"I guess mosquitoes aren't totally bad.

96
00:06:29.811 --> 00:06:34.152
And wetlands aren't useless after all," he said and ran upstairs to write his report.
